 ## Overview
-Loss function configuration defines the mathematical structure and hyperparameter weights of the total objective used to optimize radiance fields or Gaussian splatting representations. It combines appearance, geometric, and regularization terms to supervise the joint reflection-transmission model or feed-forward networks. It differs from sibling articles by focusing specifically on the *composition* of the loss equation and the *values* of the weighting coefficients, rather than the derivation of individual loss components (like Total Variation) or the broader training schedule.
+Loss Function Configuration defines the composite objective function and hyperparameter weights used to supervise the optimization of differentiable radiance fields. It combines geometric reconstruction fidelity, perceptual quality metrics, and regularization terms to guide the neural network or primitive-based representation toward the desired visual output. It differs from simple reconstruction objectives by integrating high-level perceptual metrics (SSIM, LPIPS) and specific regularization constraints (normal consistency, depth smoothness) to stabilize training and improve generalization.
 
 ## How it works
